                <pb facs="0280" n="270" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv10/pg270"/>

                <head>Appendix 141<anchor n="4" xml:id="d18-f1"/></head>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#italic">Extracts From the Minutes of the
                        Thirteenth Meeting of the Finance Section</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">15th
                        May</hi>)</p>
                <p rendition="#flush">12. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Minute</hi> 150 <hi
                        rendition="#small-caps">of the Supreme Economic Council</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#small-caps">Annex H</hi>).<anchor n="5" xml:id="d18-f2"/></p>
                <p>Mr. Keynes said that in its present form the minute of the Supreme Economic
                    Council would appear only to cover the losses that might be incurred by the
                    United States Food Administration in the case of a breach with the Germans.</p>
                <p>Mr. Davis said that he accepted a wider application of the principle, and agreed
                    that the loss arising out of a breach should be considered as a kind of cost of
                    the war which ought to be shared between all the Allied and Associated
                    Governments.</p>
                <p>It was agreed (1) that any loss arising out of the food supplies not delivered to
                    Germany by reason of a breakdown of negotiations is a general charge whose
                    distribution must be agreed between the Allied and Associated Governments when,
                    and if, the circumstances arise; (2) that the Finance Section recommend to the
                    Supreme Economic Council that in this event the German ships should be held as
                    security to cover the loss.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
